625, -0.019317626953125, 0.032623291015625, 0.00649261474609375, 0.006683349609375, 0.04669189453125, -0.02215576171875, -0.004802703857421875, 0.0017414093017578125, 0.0070648193359375, 0.01885986328125, 0.0080718994140625, 0.034912109375, -0.0295257568359375, 0.044097900390625, -0.0019311904907226562, 0.01361083984375, -0.02020263671875, 0.008056640625, -0.017120361328125, -0.019134521484375, 0.01175689697265625, 0.01213836669921875, -0.01329803466796875, 0.0306854248046875, 0.033355712890625, -0.0118865966796875, -0.0094146728515625, -0.0008378028869628906, 0.0148162841796875, -0.004901885986328125, -0.0081787109375, 0.04302978515625, 0.0225677490234375, -0.0009546279907226562, -0.0296173095703125, -0.0097808837890625, -0.01369476318359375, -0.01137542724609375, -0.0290069580078125, 0.004734039306640625, -0.031036376953125, 0.002288818359375, -0.013427734375, 0.00489044189453125, 0.0261688232421875, 0.0137176513671875, 0.0014486312866210938, -0.0081329345703125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\"\"\"\n# Introduction\n\nLarge-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, conventional pipelines typically optimize task-specific datasets in isolation, without considering the relationships between different generative tasks. A central challenge is not only how to curate each task-specific corpus, but also how to organize heterogeneous supervision according to the dependencies among generative capabilities.\n\n## Capabilities vs. Tasks\n\nWe define a **capability** as a distinct, transferable generative skill that a model can acquire and apply across multiple tasks. Formally, a capability \\( C \\) is a function or module that enables the model to perform a class of generative transformations, parameterized by input and output modalities (e.g., text-to-image, image-to-image, image-to-knowledge). Capabilities are generalizable and reusable across tasks.\n\nA **task** is a specific instance or application that exercises one or more capabilities. Formally, a task \\( T \\) is a tuple \\((C, x, y)\\), where \\(C\\) is the required capability (or set of capabilities), \\(x\\) is the input, and \\(y\\) is the desired output. Tasks are concrete, context-dependent, and used for both training and evaluation.\n\n**Concrete Examples:**\n- *Text-to-Image (T2I) Synthesis* (Capability): The ability to generate an image from a textual description.\n    - Task: \"Generate an image of a golden retriever playing in a park.\"\n- *Image Editing* (Capability): The ability to modify an existing image according to a textual or visual instruction.\n    - Task: \"Add a red sofa to a living room image.\" (Requires both T2I and image editing capabilities.)\n- *Image-Knowledge Association* (Capability): The ability to link an image to structured knowledge entities (e.g., object categories, factual attributes, or external knowledge graphs).\n    - Task: \"Given an image of a pangolin climbing a tree, associate it with the knowledge entity 'Pangolin (Mammal)' and its conservation status.\"\n\n| Aspect           | Capability (e.g., T2I, Editing, Knowledge Association) | Task (e.g., Generate, Edit, Associate)         |\n|------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| Definition       | Generalizable generative skill                        | Specific instance or application              |\n| Scope            | Broad, reusable across tasks                          | Narrow, context-dependent                     |\n| Example          | Image editing                                         | Remove a tree from a photo                    |\n| Dependencies     | May require other capabilities                        | Requires certain capabilities                 |\n\nThese dependencies among capabilities are critical for designing effective training curricula. For example, the ability to perform image editing presupposes that the model can already generate coherent images from text. Similarly, image-knowledge association may require both T2I and editing capabilities to generate or modify images that accurately reflect knowledge entities.\n\n**Novelty and Contributions:**  \nWe introduce a capability-driven data infrastructure that systematically organizes data collection and training according to the progression of model capabilities. Unlike prior work\u2014such as DALL-E 2 [1], Imagen [2], and Parti [3]\u2014which often treat each generative task and its dataset independently, our framework explicitly models and operationalizes the dependencies among generative skills (capabilities), enabling curriculum learning that reflects these relationships. Our key contributions are:\n- A modular data infrastructure that aligns data curation and curriculum learning with the dependency structure of generative capabilities.\n- Three interoperable data engines for text-image grounding, inter-image transformation, and image-knowledge association, each tailored to a specific capability.\n- A curriculum learning protocol that dynamically schedules data and tasks based on capability dependencies and ongoing evaluation, with explicit transition criteria.\n- Capability-aware evaluation and gap-aware resampling to iteratively improve data coverage and model performance.\n- Large-scale empirical validation, demonstrating improved performance over baselines on compositional and photorealistic image generation benchmarks.\n\n# Methods\n\nWe present a capability-driven framework for organizing data and training curricula in large-scale image generation. The following sections detail the formalization of capabilities and tasks, the construction of the capability dependency graph, the design and interoperability of data engines, the role of caption experts and expert construction, the curriculum learning protocol, evaluation and resampling strategies, and quality assurance procedures.\n\n## High-Level Overview\n\nOur methodology consists of the following major components:\n1. **Capability and Task Formalization:** Defining and enumerating the set of generative capabilities and their associated tasks.\n2. **Capability Dependency Graph Construction:** Identifying dependencies among capabilities and encoding them as a directed acyclic graph (DAG).\n3. **Data Engines:** Developing interoperable engines for collecting and curating data aligned with each capability.\n4. **Caption Experts and Expert Construction:** Leveraging automated and human experts for supervision and targeted data refinement.\n5. **Curriculum Learning Protocol:** Scheduling training stages and transitions based on capability dependencies and performance thresholds.\n6. **Evaluation, Resampling, and Quality Assurance:** Monitoring model progress, identifying data gaps, and maintaining data quality at scale.\n\n**Textual Description of Figure 0:**  \nImagine a flowchart with six boxes, each representing one of the above components, connected sequentially from \"Capability and Task Formalization\" to \"Evaluation, Resampling, and Quality Assurance,\" illustrating the overall pipeline.\n\n## Formalization of Capabilities and Tasks\n\nAs defined above, a **capability** is a generalizable generative skill (e.g., T2I synthesis, image editing, image-knowledge association), while a **task** is a specific instance that exercises one or more capabilities (e.g., \"generate an image of a pangolin climbing a tree\" or \"replace the background in this image with a beach scene\"). Capabilities are the building blocks for tasks, and tasks are used to evaluate and train capabilities.\n\n### Capability Selection Criteria and Coverage\n\nCapabilities are selected based on the following criteria:\n- **Generality:** The skill must be applicable across a broad range of tasks and domains.\n- **Transferability:** The capability should support transfer to new tasks or compositions.\n- **Compositionality:** The capability can be combined with others to enable more complex tasks.\n- **Data Availability:** Sufficient data can be collected or generated to support learning the capability.\n- **Relevance:** The capability addresses a core requirement in state-of-the-art image generation benchmarks.\n\nIn this work, we focus on three core capabilities: (1) Text-to-Image (T2I) Synthesis, (2) Image Editing, and (3) Image-Knowledge Association. This set is not exhaustive but is representative of foundational and compositional generative skills. The framework is extensible: new capabilities can be added by defining their input-output modalities, identifying dependencies, and integrating them into the dependency graph and data engines.\n\n## Capability Dependency Graph Construction\n\n### Identification Process\n\nDependencies among capabilities are identified through a hybrid process:\n\n1. **Manual Task Analysis:**  \n   We analyze the input-output requirements and prerequisite knowledge for each capability. For example, image editing requires the ability to generate images from text, as edits must be rendered realistically.\n\n2. **Empirical Ablation Studies:**  \n   We conduct controlled experiments where models are trained with and without specific capabilities. For instance, we train a model with only T2I data and test its performance on editing tasks, and vice versa. Criteria for ablation include:\n   - Downstream performance drop (e.g., >10% decrease in editing accuracy when T2I is withheld).\n   - Failure to generalize to composite tasks.\n   - Human rater agreement on qualitative degradation.\n\n3. **Domain Expert Consultation:**  \n   We select domain experts based on publication record and experience in generative modeling. Disagreements among experts are resolved through structured discussions and, if necessary, majority voting. For ambiguous cases, we run additional ablation studies to empirically validate the ordering.\n\n### Encoding and Example\n\nThe resulting dependency structure is encoded as a directed acyclic graph (DAG), where nodes represent capabilities and edges indicate prerequisite relationships. For example, T2I is a prerequisite for image editing, which in turn is a prerequisite for knowledge association. The curriculum follows a topological ordering of this graph, ensuring foundational skills are mastered before introducing dependent tasks.\n\n**Textual Description of Figure 1:**  \nImagine a diagram with three nodes:  \n- \"Text-to-Image (T2I)\" at the base,  \n- \"Image Editing\" above it, with an arrow from T2I to Image Editing,  \n- \"Image-Knowledge Association\" at the top, with an arrow from Image Editing to Image-Knowledge Association.  \nThis illustrates the prerequisite relationships among capabilities.\n\n## Data Engines and Interoperability\n\nOur infrastructure is built around three specialized yet interoperable data engines, each responsible for a specific type of supervision:\n\n1. **Text-Image Grounding Engine:**  \n   Collects and curates high-quality image-caption pairs for T2I generation, ensuring diverse and accurate textual descriptions.\n\n2. **Inter-Image Transformation Engine:**  \n   Constructs paired examples for image editing tasks, such as object addition, removal, or style transfer, by generating or mining before-and-after image pairs with corresponding transformation descriptions.\n\n3. **Image-Knowledge Association Engine:**  \n   Links images to structured knowledge entities, enabling models to connect visual content with external information such as object categories or factual attributes.\n\n### Interoperability and Data Flow\n\nThe three engines are interoperable in the following ways:\n- **Shared Supervision:** Outputs from the Text-Image Grounding Engine (e.g., high-quality captions) are used as inputs for the Inter-Image Transformation Engine to describe both the original and edited images.\n- **Cross-Engine Annotation:** The Image-Knowledge Association Engine leverages captions and transformation descriptions from the other engines to provide richer context for linking images to knowledge entities.\n- **Unified Data Schema:** All engines output data in a standardized format (image, text, optional transformation"}}